    %main.sub-main
      .sub-content
        %h1= mix_h1
        %p.sub-lead= mix_lead

        %p
          This page documents the mixer layer of
          <code>src/lib/Ornithopter/Ornithopter.cpp</code> and the profile system of
          <code>OrnithopterConfig.h</code>. For the surrounding architecture, start with the
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}documentation/architecture/">Architecture article</a>;
          for pulse timing and trim ranges, see the
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}documentation/servo-kernel/">Servo Kernel</a> article.

        %h2= mix_h2_tags
        %p
          Every output of every mixer is a µs value written into <code>_f[]</code>, indexed by a
          <em>ServoFunc tag</em>. Tags are transport — they say <em>what</em> a signal means, never
          <em>where</em> it is wired:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Tag
            %th Name
            %th Meaning
          %tr
            %td
              %code 0
            %td
              %code SF_NONE
            %td Reserved — no function (first PWM slots stay free for BOOT and I2C)
          %tr
            %td
              %code 1 / 2
            %td
              %code SF_LEFT_WING / SF_RIGHT_WING
            %td Front flapping wings (waveform kernel)
          %tr
            %td
              %code 3
            %td
              %code SF_RUDDER
            %td Head/crest rudder (waveform) or fin rudder (gearbox) — yaw + roll blend
          %tr
            %td
              %code 4
            %td
              %code SF_MOTOR
            %td Brushless ESC throttle (gearbox kernel only)
          %tr
            %td
              %code 5 / 6
            %td
              %code SF_VTAIL_LEFT / SF_VTAIL_RIGHT
            %td V-tail surfaces, elevon-mixed (gearbox kernel)
          %tr
            %td
              %code 7
            %td
              %code SF_ELEVATOR
            %td Traditional separate elevator (gearbox profiles 6/7)
          %tr
            %td
              %code 8 / 9
            %td
              %code SF_BACK_LEFT_WING / SF_BACK_RIGHT_WING
            %td Rear wing pair of a 4-wing airframe (mirror of the front pair, own trim)
        %p
          A runtime <em>profile</em> provides the <code>funcMap</code>: an array that maps PWM
          output index → ServoFunc. The first three slots (PWM 1…3) are always
          <code>SF_NONE</code> — they belong to the BOOT strap and the I2C bus — so real servo
          functions begin at PWM index 3 (the firmware comment spells this out: index 3 = CH4,
          4 = CH5, 5 = CH6).

        %h2= mix_h2_profiles
        %p
          Eight mixer profiles exist. Profiles 0–2 drive the <em>waveform</em> kernel (flapping
          wings), profiles 3–7 the <em>gearbox</em> kernel (motor + tails):
        %table
          %tr
            %th #
            %th Profile
            %th Kernel
            %th Servos
            %th funcMap (PWM 3 · 4 · 5)
          %tr
            %td
              %code 0
            %td
              %code SERVO_2WING
            %td waveform
            %td 2
            %td Left wing · Right wing · —
          %tr
            %td
              %code 1
            %td
              %code SERVO_2WING_1RUD
            %td waveform
            %td 3
            %td Left wing · Right wing · Rudder
          %tr
            %td
              %code 2
            %td
              %code SERVO_4WING
            %td waveform
            %td 4 (back-right shares the back-left mirror)
            %td Left wing · Right wing · Back-left wing
          %tr
            %td
              %code 3
            %td
              %code GEARBOX_2VTAIL_1RUD
            %td gearbox
            %td 3
            %td Rudder · V-tail left · V-tail right
          %tr
            %td
              %code 4
            %td
              %code GEARBOX_1MOT_2VTAIL
            %td gearbox
            %td 3
            %td Motor · V-tail left · V-tail right
          %tr
            %td
              %code 5
            %td
              %code GEARBOX_1MOT_2VTAIL_1RUD
            %td gearbox
            %td 4 (V-tail right shares the left mirror)
            %td Rudder · Motor · V-tail left
          %tr
            %td
              %code 6
            %td
              %code GEARBOX_1ELE_1RUD
            %td gearbox
            %td 2
            %td Rudder · Elevator · —
          %tr
            %td
              %code 7
            %td
              %code GEARBOX_1MOT_1ELE_1RUD
            %td gearbox
            %td 3
            %td Rudder · Motor · Elevator
        %p
          Selection: build-time flag <code>MIXER_PROFILE</code> (legacy
          <code>ORNITHOPTER_GEARBOX=1</code> → profile 5), then runtime-overridable through the
          WebUI (<code>setOrnithopterProfile</code>). The kernel test is simply
          <code>activeProfile &ge; GEARBOX_2VTAIL_1RUD</code>.

        %h2= mix_h2_wing
        %p
          All channel values enter the mixers normalized: <code>_crsfToNorm</code> maps the raw
          172…1811 window linearly onto −1…+1. Steering commands are then scaled by the
          per-profile authority sliders (<code>aileronScale</code>, <code>elevatorScale</code>, 0–100%)
          times ±60° (<code>ORNI_STEER_MAX_DEG</code>).
        %p
          <strong>Mirror geometry.</strong> Wing servos are mirror-mounted, which swaps the meaning
          of common-mode and differential terms. The code therefore treats aileron (roll) as
          <em>common</em> — it adds to both wings, which becomes a differential <em>physical</em>
          deflection — and elevator (pitch) as <em>differential</em> in code, which becomes a
          common physical deflection. The angle equations, with <code>ORNI_NEUTRAL_ANGLE_DEG = 100°</code>
          and the 2× multiplier applied to deviation terms only:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              angleLeft  = NEUTRAL + (aileron + elevator + flapCentre − degL) · 2
              angleRight = NEUTRAL + (aileron − elevator − flapCentre + degR) · 2

              flapping:  degL/R = amplitudeL/R · shapedWave(rawPhase, ferocityL/R, …)
              glide:     degL = degR = 0 → static glide offset takes over
        %p
          <code>degL</code>/<code>degR</code> are the instantaneous stroke deflections of the left
          and right wing, each the product of its own amplitude and a ferocity-shaped wave.
          Elevator-up strengthens the <em>downstroke</em>; elevator-down the <em>upstroke</em>
          (asymmetric ferocity). Yaw splits the wings in two complementary ways:
        %ul
          %li
            <strong>Ferocity differential</strong> (<code>rudderFerocityRange</code>): left gets
            <code>+ferocity</code>, right <code>−ferocity</code> — the two wings dwell differently
            but keep a <em>shared reversal phase</em> (<code>limiarShared</code>), so turns are
            pure roll torque, never desynchronisation.
          %li
            <strong>Amplitude differential</strong> (<code>rudderAmplitudeDifferential</code>):
            left amplitude ×(1+Δ), right ×(1−Δ) — symmetric divergence around the throttle-set
            stroke.
        %p
          Angles are clamped 0…180° and converted to pulses with the configurable envelope
          <code>servoMinUs…servoMaxUs</code> (defaults 988…2012): µs = min + angle·(max−min)/180,
          plus the per-function trim. The glide/flap centre offsets (<code>glideAngleDeg</code>,
          <code>flappingAngleDeg</code>, ±15°) enter as static terms on their respective branch —
          so glide and flap centres can be tuned independently per flight profile.

        %h2= mix_h2_rudder
        %p
          The rudder mixer is <em>shared verbatim by both kernels</em> — one equation, two
          airframes:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              mix  = rudderNorm · rudderYawWeight  +  aileronNorm · rudderRollWeight
              mix  = clamp(mix, −1 … +1)
              µs   = 1500 + mix · 500            (rudder centre ± 500 µs)
                     + gyroRudderCorrection       (Zephyrus, when enabled)
                     + servoTrimUs[SF_RUDDER]
        %p
          Defaults: <code>rudderYawWeight = 65%</code>, <code>rudderRollWeight = 35%</code>
          (runtime-tunable in the WebUI, stored per backup). Roll coupling on the rudder is what
          lets a wing-heavy pterosaur hold coordinated turns without yaw-string gymnastics; reduce
          the roll weight if the head rudder feels too aggressive in roll.

        %h2= mix_h2_gearbox
        %p
          The gearbox kernel runs statelessly every tick. Its three sub-mixers:
        %ul
          %li
            <strong>Motor</strong> — <code>SF_MOTOR</code>: when armed, throttle 0…1 maps to
            1000…2000&nbsp;µs; disarmed or link-down forces the minimum (motor stopped).
          %li
            <strong>Rudder</strong> — identical equation to the section above.
          %li
            <strong>Tails</strong> — two families. Classic profiles (6/7) drive a separate
            elevator: <code>µs = 1500 + elevatorNorm·500 + pitchPID + trim</code>. V-tail profiles
            (3/4/5) elevon-mix both channels onto the two tail servos:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              VtailLeft  = 1500 + (aileron + elevator)·scale + rollPID + pitchPID + trimL
              VtailRight = 1500 + (aileron − elevator)·scale − rollPID + pitchPID + trimR
              scale = 500 µs;  both aileron/elevator sums clamped to −1…+1 first
        %p
          On the gearbox kernel, Zephyrus injects <em>both</em> roll and pitch PID corrections into
          the tail servos (roll anti-phase, pitch common-mode), each hard-clamped to
          ±250&nbsp;µs (<code>ZEPHYR_GEARBOX_CLAMP_US</code>). A gyro glitch can therefore
          waggle, but never stall, a tail servo.

        %h2= mix_h2_gyro
        %p
          Three different injection points exist depending on kernel and axis:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Axis
            %th Waveform kernel
            %th Gearbox kernel
          %tr
            %td Rudder / yaw
            %td <code>gyroRudderCorrection</code> added to the crest rudder µs
            %td <code>gyroRudderCorrection</code> added to the fin rudder µs
          %tr
            %td Roll (aileron)
            %td — (waveform wings carry no roll PID)
            %td <code>gyroAileronCorrection</code>, anti-phase across V-tail, clamped ±250&nbsp;µs
          %tr
            %td Pitch (elevator)
            %td — (injected as wave modulation in the Zephyrus layer)
            %td <code>gyroElevatorCorrection</code>, common-mode, clamped ±250&nbsp;µs
        %p
          <strong>Trims &amp; limits.</strong> Every function has a signed per-servo trim
          (<code>servoTrimUs[tag]</code>, default 0, ±300&nbsp;µs) added last, and every value
          passes <code>_clampServo</code> — an absolute 500…2500&nbsp;µs safety clamp that
          outranks everything. On failsafe or link-down, all functions return to 1500&nbsp;µs and a
          gearbox motor to minimum, so the model always glides to neutral.
        %p
          Ready for the practical layer? The
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}tutorials/waveform-tuning/">Waveform Tuning</a>
          tutorial shows how these numbers feel from the sticks, and the
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}documentation/architecture/">Architecture</a> article
          shows where each mixer sits in the pipeline.
